<!--
  Launcher icon, foreground layer. Original work, Copyright (C) 2026 Eyed.

  The mark: a prompt chevron and a block cursor — `>` and the cell the caret
  sits in. It says "you can type here, on this phone" in two shapes, which is
  the whole product, and it is drawn from the most generic glyphs in computing,
  so it carries nobody's trademark. It replaces the Android robot head this
  file used to hold.

  Everything else was rejected in front of a renderer rather than in the
  abstract, at 48dp on both a light and a dark backdrop, circle- and
  squircle-masked:

  * `>` alone reads as a play button or a "next" affordance, not as an IDE.
  * `>` plus a vertical bar reads as the media skip-to-end control `>|`.
  * `>` plus an underscore is the terminal cliche, and at 48dp the underscore
    thins to a dash that the mask crowds against the chevron.

  The block cursor wins because it is a *cell*, taller than it is wide, which
  is a text cursor and not a square button, and because it is the only element
  carrying colour — an amber caret against a bone prompt. Amber is deliberate
  distance: Android's green, Solana's violet-to-green gradient and the blues
  every other dev tool uses are all somewhere else on the wheel.

  Geometry, since it is load-bearing and someone will want to re-cut it. The
  adaptive canvas is 108x108, the launcher shows the middle 72x72, and every
  mask shape fits inside the middle 66x66 circle. Ink here spans x 31.2..76.8
  and y 31.6..76.4 — 45.6 x 44.8, dead centred on (54,54) — and its furthest
  point from the centre is 30.2, so it clears the 33-unit safe radius by 2.8
  units on every mask. That is about 63% of the visible tile: full enough to
  carry at 48dp, inset enough that the squircle does not crop the chevron's
  round caps.

  The chevron is a stroke, not a filled wedge, so its weight (8.4 units, ~5.6dp
  at a 48dp render) is one number to tune rather than an outline to redraw, and
  its 3:4 width-to-height ratio makes it the typographic `>` rather than the
  equilateral arrowhead a 45-degree chevron gives you.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="108dp"
    android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108">
    <path
        android:pathData="M35.4,35.8 L49,54 L35.4,72.2"
        android:strokeColor="#FFF4EFE3"
        android:strokeWidth="8.4"
        android:strokeLineCap="round"
        android:strokeLineJoin="round"
        android:fillColor="#00000000" />
    <path
        android:pathData="M65.2,43.2 L73.4,43.2 A3.4,3.4 0 0 1 76.8,46.6 L76.8,61.4 A3.4,3.4 0 0 1 73.4,64.8 L65.2,64.8 A3.4,3.4 0 0 1 61.8,61.4 L61.8,46.6 A3.4,3.4 0 0 1 65.2,43.2 Z"
